course: biology
lesson: 191
title: Myths and cartoons put right: Tom and Jerry, elephants and mice, ostriches and more
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cartoons and old sayings are full of animals doing things real animals never do: an elephant leaps onto a chair at the sight of a mouse, an ostrich hides its head in the sand, a snake hypnotises a rabbit with its stare.
explain: Most of these myths start from something real that was seen wrongly: an ostrich lying flat with its neck on the ground looks headless from far away, and a snake that cannot blink seems to stare.
explain: Think of a game of whispers at school: the first child says something true, and by the end of the line it has turned into nonsense. Animal myths travel the same way, from storytellers to cartoon makers.
explain: To put a myth right, ask what the animal really sees, eats, hears or needs, and look for the true thing hiding inside the story.
explain: Tom chasing Jerry wildly round the house is fun, but a real cat hunts by waiting still and pouncing in one short burst.
fact: Real cats do hunt mice, but many well-fed house cats are lazy hunters, and some are even scared of rats.
fact: Cats hunt by waiting, creeping and pouncing, not by long wild chases like Tom's; farm cats can catch many mice in a year.
fact: There is no good evidence that elephants are especially afraid of mice; they can be startled by any small thing that suddenly moves near their feet.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in the sand; to hide they lie flat with their necks along the ground, and they also turn their eggs in the nest with their beaks.
fact: Bulls see red poorly and charge at a matador's cape (the cloth a bullfighter waves) because it moves, whatever its colour.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months; they have been trained to push levers, swim through mazes and come to one corner at feeding time.
fact: Porcupines cannot shoot their quills; the quills come off easily when touched and stick into the attacker, and lions and leopards are sometimes badly hurt by them.
fact: Bears break into bees' nests mostly for the grubs, which are full of fat and protein; most bears eat mainly plants, berries and nuts.
fact: Most birds have a poor sense of smell and will not abandon a chick because a person touched it; a fledgling (a young bird just learning to fly) on the ground usually has its parents nearby and is best left alone.
fact: Cutting an earthworm in half does not make two worms; sometimes the front part regrows a tail, but the back part usually dies, while some flatworms can regrow from many small pieces.
fact: Wolves do not howl at the moon; they lift their noses so the howl travels further, to call the pack and warn other packs away.
fact: Foxes are clever, but crows, ravens and parrots solve puzzles that need several steps; cartoon personalities are made up.
fact: Snakes cannot hypnotise animals; they have no eyelids, so they stare without blinking, and a snake charmer's snake follows the moving pipe, not the music, because snakes cannot hear tunes in the air the way we do.
exercise: How does a real cat usually catch a mouse? | It waits still, creeps up and pounces in one short burst | It chases it round and round the house for minutes until the mouse gets tired | It sets a trap with cheese | It barks loudly until the mouse comes out of its hole
exercise: Are all pet cats keen mouse hunters? | No, many well-fed cats hunt little, and some are scared of rats | Yes, every pet cat catches at least one mouse a day, however well it is fed | No, cats never eat mice at all | Yes, but only black cats, because they hide better in the dark
exercise: Is it true that elephants are terrified of mice? | There is no good evidence; any sudden small movement near their feet may startle them | Yes, a mouse can run up an elephant's trunk and tickle it so badly it cannot breathe | Yes, elephants faint when they see one | Yes, mice nibble elephants' toenails at night, so elephants learn to fear them
exercise: What does an ostrich really do to hide from danger? | It lies flat with its neck along the ground | It buries its head in the sand | It climbs a tree | It digs a burrow and jumps in
exercise: Why might people far away think an ostrich has its head in the sand? | It lies flat or turns its eggs with its beak, so its head looks hidden | It really puts its head underground for an hour at a time to keep cool and safe | It sleeps with its head in a hole | Its head is so small compared with its body that it seems to vanish from far off
exercise: What makes a bull charge at the matador's cape? | The movement of the cape | The red colour of the cape | The smell of the red cape | The noise the crowd makes
exercise: How long can a goldfish remember something? | For months | For three seconds | For one minute | For exactly one day
exercise: What happens when an animal touches a porcupine? | The loose quills come off and stick into it | The porcupine fires its quills like arrows at anything that comes near | The quills turn soft | The porcupine rolls up and the quills go back inside its skin
exercise: What do bears mostly want when they break into a bees' nest? | The bee grubs, full of fat and protein | Only the honey, nothing else | The wax to build their dens | The bees as pets
exercise: What do most bears eat most of the time? | Plants, berries and nuts | Honey, all day and every day | Only fish caught from rivers | Only other bears' leftovers
exercise: You touched a baby bird. Will its parents leave it? | No, most birds hardly smell and do not abandon chicks | Yes, always, because they smell your hand on it and think it is not theirs | Yes, unless you wear gloves next time | Only if it is a pigeon or a sparrow
exercise: What usually happens if an earthworm is cut in two? | The front part may regrow a tail, but the back part usually dies | Two healthy worms crawl off, each one with a new head and a new tail | Both halves grow new heads | Both halves find each other and glue back together
exercise: Why do wolves point their noses up when they howl? | So the sound carries further to the pack | To talk to the moon, which wolves think is another wolf | To smell the stars | To swallow the cold night air before they sleep
exercise: Which animals do best on puzzles that need several steps? | Crows, ravens and parrots | Foxes, which beat all other animals | Goldfish | Ostriches
exercise: Why does a snake seem to stare and hypnotise? | It has no eyelids, so it cannot blink | It has magic eyes | It is asleep with its eyes open | Its eyes spin round
exercise: What does a charmer's snake follow when it sways? | The movement of the pipe | The tune of the sweet music | The smell of the snake charmer | The loud beat of the drum
test: «tom's been chasing jerry round that house 80 years and never got him, cats are rubbish hunters innit» What is right? | Real cats hunt well, but by waiting and pouncing, not long chases | Right, real cats can never catch mice because mice are too clever | Right, mice are faster than cats in every way, even over a short jump | Real cats only hunt cheese
test: In a cartoon an elephant shrieks and jumps on a stool when a mouse runs past. What is the best correction? | No good evidence elephants fear mice; any sudden small movement might startle them | Elephants really are terrified of mice and can jump onto stools to escape them | Mice are the only animal elephants fear | Elephants squash every mouse they see, so they have nothing to fear from them at all
test: Count them: 3 ostriches stand in a row. How many legs and how many toes, if each foot has 2 toes? | 6 legs and 12 toes | 6 legs and 6 toes | 12 legs and 12 toes | 3 legs and 6 toes
test: A scientist waves a still red flag and a flapping green flag at a bull. Which will it most likely charge? | The flapping green flag | The still bright red flag | Neither, bulls only charge people | Both at the very same moment
test: «my goldfish swims to the corner every morning when i open the curtains, coincidence?» What is most likely? | It has learned that food comes then, because goldfish remember for months | Coincidence, everybody knows goldfish forget everything in three seconds | It is scared of the light | It is trying to escape the tank, and the corner is where the glass is thinnest
test: What would happen if a hungry leopard grabbed a porcupine? | It could end up with quills stuck painfully in its face and paws | The porcupine would shoot it from far away | Nothing, quills are soft as hair | The leopard would turn into a porcupine
test: Winnie-the-Pooh lives on honey. If a real bear raids a bees' nest, what is it really after? | Mostly the fat grubs, with the honey as well | Only honey, it throws the grubs away | The bees, to keep as pets | The wax, to make candles
test: «found a little bird hopping on the grass with feathers, I'm taking it home to save it yeah?» What is best? | No, it is probably a fledgling and its parents are nearby, so leave it | Yes, its mum will never come back for it once it has fallen out of the nest | Yes, birds can't live on the ground at all | Yes, but wash it gently in soap and warm water first
test: «cut a worm with my spade, now I got 2 worms lol» What is right? | No, at best the front half lives and regrows a tail; the back half usually dies | Yes, two worms for the price of one, because each half grows whatever it is missing | Yes, and each half grows a new head | Yes, worms split on purpose
test: Count them: a pack of 4 wolves howls on a hill. How many tails and how many ears? | 4 tails and 8 ears | 8 tails and 8 ears | 4 tails and 4 ears | 4 tails and 16 ears
test: In The Jungle Book the snake Kaa hypnotises Mowgli with swirling eyes. What is true? | Snakes cannot hypnotise; they just cannot blink | Real pythons hypnotise children | Only big snakes can hypnotise | Snakes hypnotise with their tongues
test: «that fox in the cartoon outsmarts everyone, so foxes are the cleverest animals ever» What is the best answer? | Foxes are clever, but crows and parrots solve harder puzzles; cartoons invent personalities | Yes, foxes beat every other animal at every test, just like in the cartoons and the old fables | No, foxes are the least clever mammals | Yes, foxes can talk and read
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment behaviour of the ostrich. | The ostrich lies prostrate with its neck extended along the ground | The ostrich inserts its head beneath the surface of the sand until the danger has passed | The ostrich ascends the nearest tree | The ostrich excavates a burrow and enters it
exam: Identify the stimulus to which a bull principally responds when it charges the cape of the matador. | The movement of the cloth | The red colour of the cloth | The odour of the coloured cloth | The rough texture of the cloth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the memory of the goldfish? | It is capable of retaining learned behaviour for a period of months | It is incapable of retaining any information beyond three seconds | It retains information only in darkness | It retains information exclusively concerning colour
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a snake is popularly, though erroneously, believed to hypnotise its prey. | It possesses no eyelids and therefore stares without blinking | It emits a high sound which is inaudible to its prey and which causes it to stand quite still | It rotates its eyes in a spiral | It releases a vapour that induces sleep
